1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,710
Hi, Joanna, good to meet you.

2
00:00:03,710 --> 00:00:06,782
Now, before we discuss your new research project,

3
00:00:06,782 --> 00:00:13,310
I'd like to hear something about the psychology study you did last year for your Master's degree.

4
00:00:13,310 --> 00:00:17,220
So how did you choose your subjects for that?

5
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:23,980
Well, I had six subjects, all professional musicians, and all female.

6
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:28,880
Three were violinists and there was also a cello player

7
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,760
and a pianist and a flute player.

8
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:35,007
They were all very highly regarded in the music world

9
00:00:35,007 --> 00:00:37,929
and they'd done quite extensive tours in different continents,

10
00:00:37,929 --> 00:00:41,500
and quite a few had won prizes and competitions as well.

11
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,870
And they were quite young, weren't they?

12
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:50,870
Yes, between 25 and 29 - the mean was 27.8.

13
00:00:50,870 --> 00:00:54,328
I wasn't specifically looking for artists who'd produced recordings,

14
00:00:54,328 --> 00:01:00,090
but this is something that's just taken for granted these days, and they all had.

15
00:01:00,090 --> 00:01:05,260
Right. Now you collected your data through telephone interviews, didn't you?

16
00:01:05,260 --> 00:01:13,870
Yes. I realised if I was going to interview leading musicians it'd only be possible over the phone because they're so busy.

17
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:17,830
I recorded them using a telephone recording adaptor.

18
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:22,670
I'd been worried about the quality, but it worked out all right.

19
00:01:22,670 --> 00:01:28,800
I managed at least a 30-minute interview with each subject, sometimes longer.

20
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:32,280
Did doing it on the phone make it more stressful?

21
00:01:32,280 --> 00:01:37,200
I'd thought it might... it was all quite informal

22
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,280
though and in fact they seemed very keen to talk.

23
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:45,080
And I don't think using the phone meant I got less rich data

24
00:01:45,080 --> 00:01:46,720
rather the opposite in fact.

25
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:52,700
Interesting. And you were looking at how performers dress for concert performances?

26
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:57,915
That's right. My research investigated the way players see their role as a musician

27
00:01:57,915 --> 00:02:03,130
and how this is linked to the type of clothing they decide to wear.

28
00:02:03,130 --> 00:02:06,680
But that focus didn't emerge immediately.

29
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:14,103
When I started I was more interested in trying to investigate the impact of what was worn on those listening,

30
00:02:14,103 --> 00:02:19,671
and also whether someone like a violinist might adopt a different style of clothing from,

31
00:02:19,671 --> 00:02:22,640
say, someone playing the flute or the trumpet.

32
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:27,310
It's interesting that the choice of dress is up to the individual, isn't it?

33
00:02:27,310 --> 00:02:32,700
Yes, you'd expect there to be rules about it in orchestras, but that's quite rare.

34
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:38,980
You only had women performers in your study

35
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:40,520
Was that because male musicians are less worried about fashion?

36
00:02:40,520 --> 00:02:46,168
I think a lot of the men are very much influenced by fashion,

37
00:02:46,168 --> 00:02:50,947
but in social terms the choices they have are more limited...

38
00:02:50,947 --> 00:02:56,160
they'd really upset audiences if they strayed away from quite narrow boundaries.

39
00:02:56,160 --> 00:03:00,750
Hmm. Now, popular music has quite different expectations.

40
00:03:00,750 --> 00:03:06,240
Did you read Mike Frost's article about the dress of women performers in popular music?

41
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:06,870
No.

42
00:03:06,870 --> 00:03:10,335
He points out that a lot of female singers

43
00:03:10,335 --> 00:03:13,200
and musicians in popular music tend to dress down in performances

44
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,495
and wear less feminine clothes,

45
00:03:16,495 --> 00:03:18,360
like jeans instead of skirts

46
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:23,040
and he suggests this is because otherwise they'd just be discounted trivial.

47
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,289
But you could argue they're just wearing what's practical...

48
00:03:26,289 --> 00:03:30,260
I mean, a pop-music concert is usually a pretty energetic affair.

49
00:03:30,260 --> 00:03:33,670
Yes, he doesn't make that point, but I think you're probably right.

50
00:03:33,670 --> 00:03:38,500
I was interested by the effect of the audience at a musical performance

51
00:03:38,500 --> 00:03:40,880
when it came to the choice of dress.

52
00:03:40,880 --> 00:03:43,970
The subjects I interviewed felt this was really important.

53
00:03:43,970 --> 00:03:48,550
It's all to do with what we understand by performance as a public event.

54
00:03:48,550 --> 00:03:51,692
They believed the audience had certain expectations

55
00:03:51,692 --> 00:03:57,340
and it was up to them as performers to fulfil these expectations

56
00:03:57,340 --> 00:03:59,770
to show a kind of esteem...

57
00:03:59,770 --> 00:04:03,070
... they weren't afraid of looking as if they'd made an effort to look good.

58
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:08,862
Mmm. I think in the past the audience would have had those expectations of one another too,

59
00:04:08,862 --> 00:04:12,950
but that's not really the case now, not in the UK anyway.

60
00:04:12,950 --> 00:04:13,520
No.

61
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:19,410
And I also got interested in what sports scientists are doing too, with regard to clothing.

62
00:04:19,410 --> 00:04:21,539
Musicians are quite vulnerable physically,

63
00:04:21,539 --> 00:04:27,074
aren't they, because the movements they carry out are very intensive and repetitive,

64
00:04:27,074 --> 00:04:36,440
so I'd imagine some features of sports clothing could safeguard the players from the potentially dangerous effects of this sort of thing.

65
00:04:36,440 --> 00:04:40,320
Yes, but musicians don't really consider it.

66
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:44,040
They avoid clothing that obviously restricts their movements

67
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:45,940
but that's as far as they go.

68
00:04:45,940 --> 00:04:48,740
Anyway, coming back to your own research

69
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:51,160
do you have any idea where you're going from here?

70
00:04:51,160 --> 00:04:57,240
I was thinking of doing a study using an audience, including...
